@node Formal syntax and semantics
@chapter Formal syntax and semantics
@node Formal syntax
@section Formal syntax

This section provides a formal syntax for Scheme written in an extended
@acronym{BNF,Backus-Naur Form}.

All spaces in the grammar are for legibility.  Case is not significant
except in the definitions of @angle{letter}, @angle{character name} and
@angle{mnemonic escape}; for example, @code{#x1A} and @code{#X1a} are
equivalent, but @code{foo} and @code{Foo} and @code{#\space} and
@code{#\Space} are distinct.  @angle{empty} stands for the empty string.

@c The following extensions to BNF are used to make the description
@c more concise: @arbno{@svar{thing}} means zero or more occurrences
@c of @svar{thing}; and @atleastone{@svar{thing}} means at least one
@c @svar{thing}.

@c @node Lexical structure
@c @subsection Lexical structure

@c @cindex token

@c This section describes how individual tokens (identifiers, numbers,
@c etc.) are formed from sequences of characters.  The following sections
@c describe how expressions and programs are formed from sequences
@c of tokens.

@c @svar{Intertoken space} can occur on either side of any token, but
@c not within a token.

@c Identifiers that do not begin with a vertical line are terminated by
@c a @svar{delimiter} or by the end of the input.  So are dot, numbers,
@c characters, and booleans.  Identifiers that begin with a vertical
@c line are terminated by another vertical line.

@c The following four characters from the ASCII repertoire are reserved
@c for future extensions to the language: @code{[ ] @{ @}}

@c In addition to the identifier characters of the ASCII repertoire
@c specified below, Scheme implementations may permit any additional
@c repertoire of Unicode characters to be employed in identifiers,
@c provided that each such character has a Unicode general category of Lu,
@c Ll, Lt, Lm, Lo, Mn, Mc, Me, Nd, Nl, No, Pd, Pc, Po, Sc, Sm, Sk, So,
@c or Co, or is U+200C or U+200D (the zero-width non-joiner and joiner,
@c respectively, which are needed for correct spelling in Persian, Hindi,
@c and other languages).  However, it is an error for the first character
@c to have a general category of Nd, Mc, or Me.  It is also an error to
@c use a non-Unicode character in symbols or identifiers.

@c All Scheme implementations must permit the escape sequence
@c @code{\x}@svar{hexdigits}@code{;} to appear in Scheme identifiers
@c that are enclosed in vertical lines. If the character with the given
@c Unicode scalar value is supported by the implementation, identifiers
@c containing such a sequence are equivalent to identifiers containing
@c the corresponding character.

@c @cindex identifier
@c @cindex comment

@c @c TODO: Better alignment in output.
@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{token} @expansion{} @svar{identifier} | @svar{boolean}
@c     | @svar{number} | @svar{character} | @svar{string}
@c     | @code{(} | @code{)} | @code{@hashchar{}(} | @code{@hashchar{}u8}
@c     | @code{'}
@c     | @code{`} | @code{,} | @code{,@@} | @code{.}

@c @svar{delimiter} @expansion{} @svar{whitespace} | @svar{vertical line}
@c     | @code{(} | @code{)} | @code{"} | @code{;}

@c @svar{intraline whitespace} @expansion{} @svar{space or tab}

@c @svar{whitespace} @expansion{} @svar{intraline whitespace}
@c     | @svar{line ending}

@c @svar{vertical line} @expansion{} @code{|}

@c @svar{line ending} @expansion{} @svar{newline}
@c     | @svar{return} @svar{newline} | @svar{return}

@c @svar{comment} @expansion{} @code{;}
@c       @pseudoprod{all subsequent characters up to a line ending}
@c     | @svar{nested comment}
@c     | @code{@hashchar{};} @svar{intertoken space} @svar{datum}

@c @svar{nested comment} @expansion{}
@c       @code{@hashchar{}|} @svar{comment text}
@c         @arbno{@svar{comment cont}} @code{|@hashchar{}}

@c @svar{comment text} @expansion{}
@c   @pseudoprod{character sequence not containing @code{@hashchar{}|}
@c               or @code{|@hashchar{}}}

@c @svar{comment cont} @expansion{} @svar{nested comment}
@c     @svar{comment text}

@c @svar{directive} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}!fold-case}
@c     | @code{@hashchar{}!no-fold-case}

@c @end indentedblock

@c Note that it is ungrammatical to follow a @svar{directive} with
@c anything but a @svar{delimiter} or the end of file.

@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{atmosphere} @expansion{} @svar{whitespace} ∣ @svar{comment}
@c     ∣ @svar{directive}

@c @svar{intertoken space} @expansion{} @arbno{@svar{atmosphere}}

@c @end indentedblock

@c Note that @code{+i}, @code{-i} and @svar{infnan} below are exceptions
@c to the @svar{peculiar identifier} rule; they are parsed as numbers, not
@c identifiers.

@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{identifier} @expansion{} @svar{initial} @arbno{@svar{subsequent}}
@c     | @svar{vertical line} @arbno{@svar{symbol element}}
@c         @svar{vertical line}
@c     | @svar{peculiar identifier}

@c @svar{initial} @expansion{} @svar{letter} | @svar{special initial}

@c @svar{letter} @expansion{} @code{a} | @code{b} | @code{c}
@c     | @dots{} | @code{z}
@c     | @code{A} | @code{B} | @code{C} | @dots{} | @code{Z}

@c @svar{special initial} @expansion{} @code{!} | @code{$} | @code{%}
@c     | @code{&} | @code{*} | @code{/} | @code{:} | @code{<} | @code{=}
@c     | @code{>} | @code{?} | @code{^} | @code{_} | @code{~}

@c @svar{subsequent} @expansion{} @svar{initial} | @svar{digit}
@c     | @svar{special subsequent}

@c @svar{digit} @expansion{} @code{0} | @code{1} | @code{2} | @code{3}
@c     | @code{4} | @code{5} | @code{6} | @code{7} | @code{8} | @code{9}

@c @svar{hex digit} @expansion{} @svar{digit} | @code{a} | @code{b}
@c     | @code{c} | @code{d} | @code{e} | @code{f}

@c @svar{explicit sign} @expansion{} @code{+} | @code{-}

@c @svar{special subsequent} @expansion{} @svar{explicit sign} | @code{.}
@c     | @code{@@}

@c @svar{inline hex escape} @expansion{}
@c       @code{\x}@svar{hex scalar value}@code{;}

@c @svar{hex scalar value} @expansion{} @atleastone{@svar{hex digit}}

@c @svar{mnemonic escape} @expansion{} @code{\a} | @code{\b} | @code{\t}
@c     | @code{\n} | @code{\r}

@c @svar{peculiar identifier} @expansion{} @svar{explicit sign}
@c     | @svar{explicit sign} @svar{sign subsequent}
@c         @arbno{@svar{subsequent}}
@c     | @svar{explicit sign} @code{.} @svar{dot subsequent}
@c       @arbno{@svar{subsequent}}
@c     | @code{.} @svar{dot subsequent} @arbno{@svar{subsequent}}

@c @svar{dot subsequent} @expansion{} @svar{sign subsequent} | @code{.}

@c @svar{sign subsequent} @expansion{} @svar{initial} | @svar{explicit sign}
@c     | @code{@@}

@c @svar{symbol element} @expansion{}
@c     @pseudoprod{any character other than @svar{vertical line} or
@c                 @code{@backslashchar{}}}
@c     | @svar{inline hex escape} | @svar{mnemonic escape} | @code{|}

@c @svar{boolean} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}t} | @code{@hashchar{}f}
@c     | @code{@hashchar{}true}
@c     | @code{@hashchar{}false}

@c @svar{character} @expansion{} @hashchar{}\ @svar{any character}
@c     | @hashchar{}\ @svar{character name}
@c     | @hashchar{}\x@svar{hex scalar value}

@c @svar{character name} @expansion{} @code{alarm} | @code{backspace}
@c    | @code{delete} | @code{escape} | @code{newline} | @code{null}
@c    | @code{return} | @code{space} | @code{tab}

@c @svar{string} @expansion{} @code{"} @arbno{@svar{string element}} @code{"}

@c @svar{string element} @expansion{}
@c       @pseudoprod{any character other than @code{"} or
@c                   @code{@backslashchar{}}}
@c     | @svar{mnemonic escape} | @code{\"} | @code{\\}
@c     | \@arbno{@svar{intraline whitespace}}@svar{line ending}
@c       @arbno{@svar{intraline whitespace}}
@c     | @svar{inline hex escape}

@c @svar{bytevector} @expansion{} \@hashchar{}u8(@arbno{@svar{byte}})

@c @svar{byte} @expansion{} @pseudoprod{any exact integer between 0 and 255}

@c @end indentedblock

@c The following rules for @svar{num R}, @svar{complex R}, @svar{real R},
@c @svar{ureal R}, @svar{uinteger R}, and @svar{prefix R} are implicitly
@c replicated for R = 2, 8, 10, and 16. There are no rules for
@c @svar{decimal 2}, @svar{decimal 8}, and @svar{decimal 16}, which means
@c that numbers containing decimal points or exponents are always in
@c decimal radix. Although not shown below, all alphabetic characters used
@c in the grammar of numbers can appear in either upper or lower case.

@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{num @var{R}} @expansion{} @svar{prefix @var{R}}
@c     @svar{complex @var{R}}

@c @svar{complex @var{R}} @expansion{} @svar{real @var{R}}
@c     | @svar{real @var{R}} @code{@@} @svar{real @var{R}}
@c     | @svar{real @var{R}} @code{+} @svar{ureal @var{R}} @code{i}
@c     | @svar{real @var{R}} @code{-} @svar{ureal @var{R}} @code{i}
@c     | @svar{real @var{R}} @code{+ i}
@c     | @svar{real @var{R}} @code{- i}
@c     | @svar{real @var{R}} @svar{infnan} @code{i}
@c     | @code{+} @svar{ureal @var{R}} @code{i}
@c     | @code{-} @svar{ureal @var{R}} @code{i}
@c     | @svar{infnan} @code{i}
@c     | @code{+ i}
@c     | @code{- i}

@c @svar{real @var{R}} @expansion{} @svar{sign} @svar{ureal @var{R}}
@c     | @svar{infnan}

@c @svar{ureal @var{R}} @expansion{} @svar{uinteger @var{R}}
@c     | @svar{uinteger @var{R}} @code{/} @svar{uinteger @var{R}}
@c     | @svar{decimal @var{R}}

@c @svar{decimal 10} @expansion{} @svar{uinteger 10} @svar{suffix}
@c     | @code{.} @atleastone{@svar{digit 10}} @svar{suffix}
@c     | @atleastone{@svar{digit 10}} @code{.} @arbno{@svar{digit 10}}
@c       @svar{suffix}

@c @svar{uinteger @var{R}} @expansion{} @atleastone{@svar{digit @var{R}}}

@c @svar{prefix @var{R}} @expansion{} @svar{radix @var{R}} @svar{exactness}
@c     | @svar{exactness} @svar{radix @var{R}}

@c @svar{infnan} @expansion{} @code{+inf.0} | @code{-inf.0}
@c     | @code{+nan.0} | @code{-nan.0}

@c @end indentedblock

@c @sharpindex{b}
@c @sharpindex{o}
@c @sharpindex{x}
@c @sharpindex{e}
@c @sharpindex{i}

@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{suffix} @expansion{} @svar{empty}
@c     | @svar{exponent marker} @svar{sign} @atleastone{@svar{digit 10}}

@c @svar{exponent marker} @expansion{} @code{e}

@c @svar{sign} @expansion{} @svar{empty} | @code{+} | @code{-}

@c @svar{exactness} @expansion{} @svar{empty} | @code{@hashchar{}i}
@c     | @code{@hashchar{}e}

@c @svar{radix 2} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}b}

@c @svar{radix 8} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}o}

@c @svar{radix 10} @expansion{} @svar{empty} | @code{@hashchar{}d}

@c @svar{radix 16} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}x}

@c @svar{digit 2} @expansion{} @code{0} | @code{1}

@c @svar{digit 8} @expansion{} @code{0} | @code{1} | @code{2} | @code{3}
@c     | @code{4} | @code{5} | @code{6} | @code{7}

@c @svar{digit 10} @expansion{} @svar{digit}

@c @svar{digit 16} @expansion{} @svar{digit 10} | @code{a} | @code{b}
@c     | @code{c} | @code{d} | @code{e} | @code{f}

@c @end indentedblock

@c @node External representations formal
@c @subsection External representations

@c @svar{Datum} is what the read procedure (@ref{Input}) successfully
@c parses. Note that any string that parses as an @svar{expression} will
@c also parse as a @svar{datum}.

@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{datum} @expansion{} @svar{simple datum} ∣ @svar{compound datum}
@c     ∣ @svar{label} @code{=} @svar{datum} ∣ @svar{label} @code{@hashchar{}}

@c @svar{simple datum} @expansion{} @svar{boolean} ∣ @svar{number}
@c     ∣ @svar{character} ∣ @svar{string} ∣ @svar{symbol}
@c     ∣ @svar{bytevector}

@c @svar{symbol} @expansion{} @svar{identifier}

@c @svar{compound datum} @expansion{} @svar{list} ∣ @svar{vector}
@c     ∣ @svar{abbreviation}

@c @svar{list} @expansion{} @code{(}@arbno{@svar{datum}}@code{)}
@c     ∣ @code{(}@atleastone{@svar{datum}} @code{.} @svar{datum}@code{)}

@c @svar{abbreviation} @expansion{} @svar{abbrev prefix} @svar{datum}

@c @svar{abbrev prefix} @expansion{} @code{'} ∣ @code{`} ∣ @code{,}
@c     ∣ @code{,@@}

@c @svar{vector} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}(}@arbno{@svar{datum}}@code{)}

@c @svar{label} @expansion{} @code{@hashchar{}} @svar{uinteger 10}

@c @end indentedblock

@c @node Expressions formal
@c @subsection Expressions

@c The definitions in this and the following subsections assume that all
@c the syntax keywords defined in this report have been properly imported
@c from their libraries, and that none of them have been redefined or
@c shadowed.

@c @indentedblock

@c @svar{expression} @expansion{} @svar{identifier}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{literal}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{procedure call}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{lambda expression}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{conditional}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{assignment}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{derived expression}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{macro use}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{macro block}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{includer}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{literal} @expansion{} @svar{quotation} | @svar{self-evaluating}

@c @svar{self-evaluating} @expansion{} @svar{boolean} | @svar{number}
@c     | @svar{vector} | @svar{character} | @svar{string} | @svar{bytevector}

@c @svar{quotation} @expansion{} @code{'}@svar{datum}
@c     | @code{(quote} @svar{datum}@code{)}

@c @svar{procedure call} @expansion{}
@c     @code{(}@svar{operator} @arbno{@svar{operand}}@code{)}

@c @svar{operator} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{operand} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{lambda expression} @expansion{}
@c     @code{(lambda} @svar{formals} @svar{body}@code{)}

@c @svar{formals} @expansion{} @code{(}@arbno{@svar{identifier}}@code{)}
@c     | @svar{identifier}
@c     | @code{(}@atleastone{@svar{identifier}} @code{.}
@c         @svar{identifier}@code{)}

@c @svar{body} @expansion{} @arbno{@svar{definition}} @svar{sequence}

@c @svar{sequence} @expansion{} @arbno{@svar{command}} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{command} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{conditional} @expansion{}
@c     @code{(if} @svar{test} @svar{consequent} @svar{alternate}@code{)}

@c @svar{test} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{consequent} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{alternate} @expansion{} @svar{expression} | @svar{empty}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{assignment} @expansion{}
@c     @code{(set!} @svar{identifier} @svar{expression}@code{)}

@c @sp 1

@c @c This is going to need some serious line-breaking.
@c @svar{derived expression} @expansion{}
@c       @code{(cond }@atleastone{@svar{cond clause}}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(cond }@arbno{@svar{cond clause}} @code{(else}
@c         @svar{sequence})@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(case }@svar{expression}
@c            @atleastone{@svar{case clause}}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(case }@svar{expression}
@c            @arbno{@svar{case clause}}
@c            @code{(else }@svar{sequence})@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(case }@svar{expression}
@c            @arbno{@svar{case clause}}
@c            @code{(else =>} @svar{recipient})@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(and }@arbno{@svar{test}}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(or }@arbno{@svar{test}}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(when }@svar{test} @svar{sequence}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(unless }@svar{test} @svar{sequence}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(let }(@arbno{@svar{binding spec}}) @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(let }@svar{identifier} (@arbno{@svar{binding spec}})
@c         @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(let* }(@arbno{@svar{binding spec}}) @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(letrec }(@arbno{@svar{binding spec}}) @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(letrec* }(@arbno{@svar{binding spec}}) @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(let-values }(@arbno{@svar{mv binding spec}})
@c         @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(let*-values }(@arbno{@svar{mv binding spec}})
@c         @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(begin }@svar{sequence}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(do }(@arbno{@svar{iteration spec}}@code{)}
@c                (@svar{test} @svar{do result}@code{)}
@c            @arbno{@svar{command}}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(delay }@svar{expression}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(delay-force }@svar{expression}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(parameterize }(@arbno{(@svar{expression}
@c         @svar{expression})}@code{)} @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(guard }(@svar{identifier} @arbno{@svar{cond clause}})
@c         @svar{body}@code{)}@*
@c @ @ | @svar{quasiquotation}@*
@c @ @ | @code{(case-lambda }@arbno{@svar{case-lambda clause}}@code{)}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{cond clause} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{test} @svar{sequence}@code{)}
@c     | @code{(}@svar{test}@code{)}
@c     | @code{(}@svar{test} @code{=>} @svar{recipient}@code{)}

@c @svar{recipient} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{case clause} @expansion{}
@c       @code{((}@arbno{@svar{datum}}@code{)} @svar{sequence}@code{)}
@c     | @code{((}@arbno{@svar{datum}}@code{) =>} @svar{recipient}@code{)}

@c @svar{binding spec} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{identifier}
@c       @svar{expression}@code{)}

@c @svar{mv binding spec} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{formals}
@c       @svar{expression}@code{)}

@c @svar{iteration spec} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{identifier} @svar{init}
@c      @svar{step}@code{)}
@c    | @code{(}@svar{identifier} @svar{init}@code{)}

@c @svar{case-lambda clause} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{formals}
@c      @svar{body}@code{)}

@c @svar{init} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{step} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{do result} @expansion{} @svar{sequence} | @svar{empty}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{macro use} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{keyword}
@c       @arbno{@svar{datum}}@code{)}

@c @svar{keyword} @expansion{} @svar{identifier}

@c @svar{macro block} @expansion{}
@c       @code{@code{(}let-syntax }@code{(}@arbno{@svar{syntax spec}}@code{)}
@c         @svar{body}@code{)}
@c     | @code{(letrec-syntax }@code{(}@arbno{@svar{syntax spec}}@code{)}
@c         @svar{body}@code{)}

@c @svar{syntax spec} @expansion{} @code{(}@svar{keyword}
@c       @svar{transformer spec}@code{)}

@c @sp 1

@c @svar{includer} @expansion{}
@c    | @code{(include }@atleastone{@svar{string}}@code{)}
@c    | @code{(include-ci }@atleastone{@svar{string}}@code{)}

@c @end indentedblock

@c @node Quasiquotations
@c @subsection Quasiquotations

@c The following grammar for quasiquote expressions is not context-free.
@c It is presented as a recipe for generating an infinite number of
@c production rules. Imagine a copy of the following rules for @var{D} =
@c 1, 2, 3, @dots{}, where @var{D} is the nesting depth.

@c @indentedblock
@c @svar{quasiquotation} @expansion{} @svar{quasiquotation 1}

@c @svar{qq template 0} @expansion{} @svar{expression}

@c @svar{quasiquotation @var{D}} @expansion{}
@c       @code{`}@svar{qq template @var{D}}
@c     | @code{(quasiquote }@svar{qq template @var{D}}@code{)}

@c @svar{qq template @var{D}} @expansion{} @svar{simple datum}
@c     | @svar{list qq template @var{D}}
@c     | @svar{vector qq template @var{D}}
@c     | @svar{unquotation @var{D}}

@c @svar{list qq template @var{D}} @expansion{}
@c       @code{(}@arbno{@svar{qq template or splice @var{D}}}@code{)}
@c     | @code{(}@atleastone{@svar{qq template or splice @var{D}}} @code{.}
@c         @svar{qq template @var{D}}@code{)}
@c     | @code{'}@svar{qq template @var{D}}
@c     | @svar{quasiquotation @var{D}+1}

@c @svar{vector qq template @var{D}} @expansion{}
@c       @code{@hashchar{}(}@arbno{@svar{qq template or
@c         splice @var{D}}}@code{)}

@c @svar{unquotation @var{D}} @expansion{}
@c       @code{,}@svar{qq template @var{D}@minus{}1}
@c     | @code{(unquote }@svar{qq template @var{D}@minus{}1}@code{)}

@c @svar{qq template or splice @var{D}} @expansion{}
@c       @svar{qq template @var{D}}
@c     | @svar{splicing unquotation @var{D}}

@c @svar{splicing unquotation @var{D}} @expansion{}
